\f1\fs72 \cf2 Test Case Prioritization using Ranking Model\

\fs36 \cf3 \
Model Type:\
This is a Ranking-based prioritization approach using a simple weighted scoring model.\
It is not a classification model.\
\
Difference:\
- Classification model \uc0\u8594  predicts category such as Defect / No Defect\
- Ranking model \uc0\u8594  gives order or priority such as which test case should run first\
\
Why this model is used:\
In QA, we usually do not only want to know whether a test case may fail or not.\
We want to know which test cases should be executed first.\
That is why ranking is more useful for test prioritization.\
\
Goal:\

\fs48 \cf4 Rank test cases based on risk so that high-risk test cases are executed first.
\fs36 \cf3 \
\
How it works:\
Each test case get\cf5 s a risk score \cf3 based on important factors.\
The h\cf6 igher the risk score,\cf3  the higher the execution priority.\
\
Factors used in this example:\
\cf7 1. Business Impact\
\cf3    - Measures how critical the feature is to the business\
   - Higher value means higher priority\
\
\cf8 2. Defect History\
\cf3    - Measures how many defects were found before in this area\
   - More past bugs means more risk\
\
\cf9 3. Change Frequency\
\cf3    - Measures how often the feature changes\
   - Frequent changes increase the chance of defects\
\
\cf8 4. Complexity\
\cf3    - Measures how complex the module or feature is\
   - Higher complexity usually means higher risk\
\

\fs48 Implementation in Python:\

\fs36 \
import pandas as pd\
\
\cf6 1-# Sample test case data\
\cf3 test_cases = pd.DataFrame(\{\
    "test_case_id": ["TC_01", "TC_02", "TC_03", "TC_04", "TC_05"],\
    "business_impact": [5, 2, 4, 1, 5],\
    "defect_history": [4, 1, 3, 0, 5],\
    "change_frequency": [5, 2, 4, 1, 3],\
    "complexity": [4, 2, 5, 1, 3]\
\})\
\
\cf9 2-# Weighted risk score calculation\cf3 \
test_cases["risk_score"] = (\
    test_cases["business_impact"] * 0.4 +\
    test_cases["defect_history"] * 0.3 +\
    test_cases["change_frequency"] * 0.2 +\
    test_cases["complexity"] * 0.1\
)\
\
\cf5 3-# Rank test cases from highest risk to lowest risk\cf3 \
ranked_test_cases = test_cases.sort_values(by="risk_score", ascending=False)\
\
\cf7 4-# Assign execution priority\
\cf3 ranked_test_cases["priority"] = range(1, len(ranked_test_cases) + 1)\
\
\cf9 # Final result\
\cf3 print(ranked_test_cases[["priority", "test_case_id", "risk_score"]])\
\

\fs48 Explanation of the formula:\

\fs36 Risk Score =\
(Business Impact \'d7 0.4) +\
(Defect History \'d7 0.3) +\
(Change Frequency \'d7 0.2) +\
(Complexity \'d7 0.1)\
\
Why these weights:\
- Business Impact has the highest weight because critical features should be tested first\
- Defect History is also important because areas with many old bugs are risky\
- Change Frequency matters because frequent updates can introduce new issues\
- Complexity matters because complex logic usually has more defect chances\
\
What the system does:\
1. Collects risk factors for each test case\
2. Calculates one score called risk_score\
3. Sorts test cases by that score\
4. Assigns priority based on sorted order\
5. Highest score becomes Priority 1\
\
Example output:\
priority  test_case_id  risk_score\
1         TC_05         4.7\
2         TC_01         4.5\
3         TC_03         3.9\
4         TC_02         1.7\
5         TC_04         0.6\
\
How to read the output:\
- TC_05 should run first because it has the highest risk score\
- TC_01 should run second\
- TC_04 can run later because it has the lowest risk\
\
Why this is useful in QA:\
- Helps execute t\cf8 he most important test cases first\cf3 \
-\cf9  Saves time w\cf3 hen execution window is limited\
- Increases c\cf9 hance of finding important defects earlie\cf3 r\
- Supports \cf8 risk-based testing approach\cf3 \
\
Simple memory sentence:\
More business impact + more old bugs + more frequent changes + more complexity = higher test priority\
\
Final summary:\
This implementation uses a simple ranking model based on weighted risk scoring.\
It helps prioritize test cases by sorting them from highest risk to lowest risk.\
This is better than classification when the goal is to decide execution order instead of only predicting pass/fail or defect/no defect.}